        "No one comes immediately. That is the last small mercy: a minute in a glass room with your own pulse and the leftover weather of whoever you allowed through the door. You use the minute to put the night in order, not as a report, as a keepsake. Dumplings or terrible tea or citrus or an unused bed. A mark on a nurse's throat or three seconds on the back of a skull or a brass lamp or a paper in a shoe. The mammal worked. The name held. The flinch survived.\n\nTomorrow — today — you will be asked to pick a genre, as Kane said. Identity. Double. Owned. Converted. Burned. The engine of the city already knows which one the numbers want. You may still walk toward a different door if your hands are stubborn. Hands have been the only honest instrument left since your mouth learned to say yes for other people.\n\nYou stand when the corridor finally sounds like a person. You do not fix your shirt. You do not hide the shine, if there is shine. You do not turn the handle. This is still the glass with no plants. The heading will come into this room; you will not go hunting it in a corridor that has already billed you. Behind you the paper cup sweats onto the blank card, a small stain that will be in the file whether they like it or not. You almost smile. You do not. Smiling is for after someone speaks, if after exists. The handle stays cold. You wait. The person in the corridor is the next chapter, and they have been waiting since the card in the locker.",
